WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.549
Speaker 1: Today we're going to be speaking
with Erica Englehart, who's a science journalist

00:00:04.549 --> 00:00:09.460
living in Washington, D.C., and also author
of the book Gory Details Adventures from the

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:11.140
Dark Side of Science.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:12.580
Erica, really great having you on.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.580
I appreciate it.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:14.580
Speaker 3: Thanks.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.580
Nice to be here.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:21.500
Speaker 1: So, you know, we've spoken over
time about everything from, you know, homeopathy

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:28.820
to drinking silver, faith, healing, all sorts
of medical treatments that may be kind of

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:29.859
worked at one point.

00:00:29.859 --> 00:00:34.879
But there are now better options compared
to out and out scams, etc..

00:00:34.879 --> 00:00:40.730
One of the things you talk about in the book
is the relation between medical myths, misconceptions

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:47.861
and irrational health fears and this sense
of disgust that as humans, we could say, has

00:00:47.861 --> 00:00:52.850
been developed or has been bred in, or how
however you sort of want to say it.

00:00:52.850 --> 00:00:54.430
Let's maybe start there.

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:59.780
Talk a little bit about the tendency towards
disgust and how it might predispose people

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.539
to some of these medical misconceptions.

00:01:01.539 --> 00:01:07.850
Speaker 3: Yeah, I think, you know, disgust
is one of those kind of overlooked emotions,

00:01:07.850 --> 00:01:13.590
something that we don't necessarily think
about or talk about a lot.

00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:17.880
But, you know, some psychologists would say
that it's one of the basic human emotions

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.060
that seems to be universal, at least the feeling
of disgust.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:27.260
You know, there are certain particular signs
of disgust that people around the world give,

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:34.009
you know, that kind of squished up face, you
know, kind of expelling something out of your

00:01:34.009 --> 00:01:35.009
body like.

00:01:35.009 --> 00:01:38.190
Speaker 1: Yeah, like 80% of the way to gagging,
we might say.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:39.540
Speaker 3: Exactly.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:40.540
Exactly.

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:44.790
And that's something that, you know, people
around the world experience that feeling of

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:45.830
disgust.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:51.790
But in terms of what things are considered
disgusting, that's really kind of up for grabs

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:52.790
culturally.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:58.310
So that is something that we we tend to learn
as we grow up.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:03.939
So there may be something where humans as
a species have evolved, the sense of disgust,

00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:08.580
probably as a protective mechanism, because
a lot of the things that disgust us are things

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:09.929
that can hurt us, right?

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:11.810
Things that could make us sick, for example.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:18.701
So, you know, we we learn we teach most of
our children around the world, you know, not

00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:19.701
to eat their poop.

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:24.410
And, you know, maybe as babies, they they're
not disgusted by poop and they'll stick their

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:26.280
hands right in it.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:33.290
But we we you know, we teach our kids to be
disgusted by the smell and appearance of poop.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:37.129
And most of us around the world get that at
some point in our lives.

00:02:37.129 --> 00:02:44.510
So but there are a lot of other things that
vary from place to place, culture to culture,

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:47.000
especially when it comes to things like food.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:52.250
So every culture has some food that it thinks
is delicious and that other cultures think

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:53.840
is totally disgusting.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:54.840
Yes.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:55.840
So that's so it's relative.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.450
So there are things that are pretty universally
disgusting.

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:05.260
Those tend to be the things that can hurt
us or make us sick.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:10.270
And then there are other things that are just,
you know, we decide basically that something

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.180
is disgusting and something is not disgusting.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.280
Speaker 1: And so then what's the connection
from that to the medical myths and misconceptions?

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.819
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:03:18.819 --> 00:03:21.990
So disgust is a really powerful feeling.

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:30.700
And so since we have this, you know, strong
aversion to certain things, that means that

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:34.790
it can really kind of guide our decisions
and shape our thinking about things.

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:41.069
And a lot of the things that we're disgusted
by involve our bodies, you know, a lot of

00:03:41.069 --> 00:03:42.819
things involving bodily functions.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:48.300
You know, all of our bodily secretions and
excretions.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.800
Blood, sweat, tears, you know, snot.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.549
These are things that you don't necessarily
you know, they're very intimate and they're

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:03.650
generally considered disgusting if they're
coming from a stranger.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:04.650
Right.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:05.650
Sure.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:11.140
So we're not necessarily disgusted so much
by our own body fluids and so forth, but we're

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.650
certainly disgusted by those of strangers.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:22.500
And so when you think about, you know, some
of the different kinds of medical treatments

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:28.000
and products, things that people are trying
to sell to us, it's very easy to play into

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:29.979
that sense of disgust.

00:04:29.979 --> 00:04:36.660
For example, there is a huge industry built
around the idea of detoxifying our bodies.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.000
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.340
Speaker 3: Purifying.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:40.340
Cleansing.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.380
All of these things are basically meant to
make us less disgusting.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.180
We all want to be less disgusting, right?

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:55.380
We all want to have this idea of being, you
know, clean and pure, but.

00:04:55.380 --> 00:05:00.420
When it comes down to it, you know, a lot
of these things are playing on our disgust

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:08.920
of our of our bodies or other people's bodies
and really are not selling us something that's

00:05:08.920 --> 00:05:09.920
real.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:17.080
You know, again, you know, it's easy to play
on that disgust and say like, oh, this is

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:19.830
going to detoxify you.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:20.919
Yeah.

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:22.539
And and that sounds good.

00:05:22.539 --> 00:05:31.740
And if we see, for example, if you you know,
there are certain kinds of sweat therapy that

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.669
that people are selling really hard.

00:05:34.669 --> 00:05:38.040
And the idea is that you can sweat out the
toxins.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.440
And if people see something coming out of
their bodies, it feels like satisfying.

00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:43.440
Right.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.440
Getting something bad out.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:45.620
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:50.550
I've told stories of I don't do it often because
I find it painfully awkward.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:54.150
But I've had like, professional massages before
and the massage goes great.

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:58.430
And at the end they say, you know, the massage
will loosen a lot of the toxins in the body,

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.069
so you should drink a lot of water.

00:06:00.069 --> 00:06:01.069
And I'm thinking, wait a second.

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:03.110
So the water is processed in the kid.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.139
What's the mechanism here that's going on?

00:06:05.139 --> 00:06:07.270
But it's sort of that same idea where that
sounds.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:08.310
It sounds generally good.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:09.310
Yeah.

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:10.380
Like, I mean, I could kind of go for that.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:15.180
You know, Jonathan Haidt has written about
the connections between disgust and political

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:16.690
leanings and predispositions.

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:17.690
Yes.

00:06:17.690 --> 00:06:23.930
And I'm wondering whether when it comes to
medical myths, misconceptions, quack cures

00:06:23.930 --> 00:06:29.400
and openness and this sort of thing, whether
there are other maybe personality traits or

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:34.650
whatever, that that we may be able to overlay
on whether it's politics or upbringing or

00:06:34.650 --> 00:06:35.650
whatever the case may be.

00:06:35.650 --> 00:06:36.849
Speaker 3: Yeah, Yeah.

00:06:36.849 --> 00:06:42.990
I mean, definitely people there's a range
and how easily disgusted people are.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:50.260
Some people are very sensitive to disgust
and are very easily triggered to feel disgusted.

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:51.410
Others not so much.

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:56.660
I seem to have a pretty high tolerance for
disgust, for example, because I write about

00:06:56.660 --> 00:06:57.789
gross things.

00:06:57.789 --> 00:06:58.789
Yes.

00:06:58.789 --> 00:07:02.199
But, you know, a lot of people don't.

00:07:02.199 --> 00:07:08.090
And there have been some studies like what
you're referencing that link those traits,

00:07:08.090 --> 00:07:14.460
how easily disgusted you are to various other
kinds of opinions that we form.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:22.900
And so some in general, I don't want to make
this any kind of hard and fast rule, but some

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:27.629
studies have found a connection between people
who are more easily disgusted and people who

00:07:27.629 --> 00:07:31.099
tend to be more conservative political leaning.

00:07:31.099 --> 00:07:32.099
Yes.

00:07:32.099 --> 00:07:38.599
And it kind of makes sense if you think about
the idea of, you know, that a person who is

00:07:38.599 --> 00:07:47.750
very easily triggered to feel disgust might
be kind of sensitive and afraid of things

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:51.310
that could cause that feeling.

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:58.100
And so things that are different, things that
are outside, you know, may be more triggering

00:07:58.100 --> 00:08:00.870
to some people than to others.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:09.280
I think the same kind of thing happens, you
know, perhaps with these kinds of health beliefs

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:11.780
and misconceptions.

00:08:11.780 --> 00:08:20.909
Some people seem to be just more sensitive
and more triggered by the idea of being contaminated

00:08:20.909 --> 00:08:26.599
by something by, you know, things that are
dirty.

00:08:26.599 --> 00:08:28.340
And that can also be.

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:31.250
Likewise, that can be played back into politically.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:32.250
You know.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:36.061
Speaker 1: One of the interesting things about
this that you're making me think of is, you

00:08:36.061 --> 00:08:42.610
know, there's a lot of I think we can all
understand how if indeed this higher sense

00:08:42.610 --> 00:08:47.420
of or this more easily triggered disgust aligns
with political conservatism, we could see

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:49.180
how that might make people against immigration.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:50.180
Right.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:54.380
We hear stories about I mean, Tucker Carlson
once overtly said immigrants make the country

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:56.870
dirtier and poorer.

00:08:56.870 --> 00:09:01.089
That's literally something that you would
be disgusted by the dirt that he said that

00:09:01.089 --> 00:09:02.440
that one's super clear.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:08.149
It's been interesting to see with the pandemic,
you would think germs around.

00:09:08.149 --> 00:09:14.769
You would you would think that with a more
easily triggered disgust response, the right

00:09:14.769 --> 00:09:19.959
would have been more sensitive to the idea
of the germs of COVID.

00:09:19.959 --> 00:09:23.810
But for political reasons and for other reasons,
it hasn't actually been the case.

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:25.260
Speaker 3: Right.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:31.450
Yeah, I think that's an interesting point,
too, you know, but disgust can cut a lot of

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:32.450
different ways.

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:35.640
And, you know, we have to remember that it
can be discussed, can also be weaponized.

00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:36.640
Right.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.459
You know, it can be turned against certain
groups of people.

00:09:40.459 --> 00:09:41.459
Like you were saying.

00:09:41.459 --> 00:09:46.060
It's often turned against immigrants saying,
oh, they're disease and dirty and they're

00:09:46.060 --> 00:09:50.640
coming in here and they're contaminating our
